 "fullarm": "Context from a materials paper:\n- High-temperature dielectric films need higher permittivity with low loss while keeping polymer strength and thermal stability\n- BST core carrying an MPTMS thiol layer decorated with Ag nanoparticles, used as hybrid filler\n- H2O2 hydroxylation of BST then MPTMS hydrolysis/coupling gives thiol-grafted BST-SH\n- MPTMS chains are covalently grafted on the BST particle surface (Si-O and CH2 groups present)\n- Interfacial adhesion, insulating MPTMS shell and Ag Coulomb blockade restrict dipole motion and space-charge build-up, suppressing loss\n- Dielectric loss stays low (text: <0.026), increasing only slightly with Ag content\n\nPanels:\n- F6b: caption: Fig. 6 Mechanical properties of pure PAEN and BST @ Ag/PAEN composites ... }$ breaking elongation\n\nQuestion: The claim under test says dielectric loss stays low (below 0.026), increasing only slightly with Ag content. Does the panel (F6b, breaking elongation/loss plot for PurePAEN, BST@Ag1%, BST@Ag3%, BST@Ag5%) support this?\n\nImages (read each with Read):\n/home/aid1/Documents/causalmat/matmech/Advanced_Composites_and_Hybrid_Materials/s42114-021-00366-2/panels/crops/e882a07abadc8f5d206ee50b35b69ee2a96baff78bb55670e0d0aec7725834ce_B.jpg\nAnswer in at most six sentences.",
